## 5. Directory Structure (Keep Updated)
<!-- ID: directory_structure -->

Planned `scribe_mcp` ownership:
- `src/scribe_mcp/utils/response.py` — additive timing carrier into the generic formatter/logging boundary.
- `src/scribe_mcp/utils/formatters/dispatcher.py` — persist real `duration_ms` and correlation metadata to JSONL and SQL.
- `src/scribe_mcp/utils/tool_logger.py` — extend JSONL schema only if needed for additive correlation fields.
- `src/scribe_mcp/runtime_timing_envelope.py` — reuse `tools.generic.phases_ms` and any additive envelope helpers.
- `src/scribe_mcp/tools/append_entry.py` — additive response timing and sub-phase instrumentation.
- `src/scribe_mcp/tools/set_project.py` — no redesign; only optional additive fields or duplicate-write optimization after proof.
- `src/scribe_mcp/log_intelligence.py` and any diagnostics/readback surface selected by implementation — consume persisted timing or report explicit missing-data state.
- `src/scribe_mcp/tools/*diagnostic*` or another existing diagnostics surface chosen during implementation — host the controlled root comparison and fresh-DB reconciliation report if the current tool inventory already has a suitable home.
- `tests/test_set_project.py`, `tests/test_log_intelligence.py`, `tests/test_doctor_telemetry.py`, `tests/test_tools.py`, plus any new focused test files — prove additive telemetry and read-only diagnostics.

Planned `council_mcp` ownership:
- `src/council_mcp/templates/defaults/hooks/registry.yaml` — authoritative hook enablement metadata if timing fields or new gate labels require registry support.
- `src/council_mcp/hook_specs.py` — source parity, runtime script resolution, and Codex hook rendering plan.
- `src/council_mcp/templates/claude/runtime_hooks/post_tool.py` — post-tool timing and fail-open instrumentation.
- `src/council_mcp/templates/claude/runtime_hooks/session_start.py` — startup/session timing attribution.
- `src/council_mcp/hooks/client.py` — measured hook logging, timeout handling, and no-fake-duration behavior.
- `src/council_mcp/compiler/phases/hooks.py` and `src/council_mcp/codex_toml.py` — install/readback and preservation behavior if hook config schema changes.
- `tests/test_hook_specs.py`, `tests/test_runtime_hook_generation.py`, `tests/test_runtime_hooks_binding.py`, `tests/test_hooks/test_hook_client.py` — source-authority, generation, binding, and timeout/fail-open proof.

---
## 6. Data & Storage
<!-- ID: data_storage -->

Authoritative data sinks remain unchanged:
- File-backed progress logs under `.scribe/docs/dev_plans/.../PROGRESS_LOG.md`
- Repo/project tool logs (`TOOL_LOG.jsonl`) via existing tool logger surfaces
- Postgres `scribe_entries`
- Postgres `tool_calls`

New or clarified additive data expectations:
- Generic tool timing rows must include a real `duration_ms` and enough correlation metadata to match the row back to a tool response or probe report.
- `append_entry` phase timing may live in response `timing`, in persisted metadata for `tool_calls`, or both, but the persisted readback must remain queryable after the transcript disappears.
- `logs analyze` must never silently imply that timing data exists when it does not. Missing-data status is a first-class result.
- Hook telemetry must remain logically separate from Scribe tool telemetry even if both ultimately land in hook logs or Council-side JSONL.

---
## 7. Testing & Validation Strategy
<!-- ID: testing_strategy -->

Validation is package-scoped and additive.

Common proof surfaces:
- `uv run pytest tests/test_set_project.py tests/test_log_intelligence.py tests/test_doctor_telemetry.py tests/test_tools.py`
- Targeted MCP probes using `mcp__scribe__set_project`, `mcp__scribe__append_entry`, `mcp__scribe__read_recent`, and `mcp__scribe__manage_docs(action="quality_check", dry_run=true)`.
- Readback proof from `TOOL_LOG.jsonl`, Postgres `tool_calls`, and `scribe_entries`.

Council-side proof surfaces:
- `uv run pytest tests/test_hook_specs.py tests/test_runtime_hook_generation.py tests/test_runtime_hooks_binding.py tests/test_hooks/test_hook_client.py`
- `council update --dry-run` readback for source-authority/install packages only.
- Generated-output readback after operator-approved generation, never hand-edited output files.

Validation rules:
- Every package that other packages depend on requires package-specific Crucible PASS before routing dependents.
- Controlled timing comparisons must be run against the same Scribe server instance and must label hook inclusion state.
- Reconciliation diagnostics must be read-only and must show no mutation side effects.

---
## 8. Deployment & Operations
<!-- ID: deployment_operations -->

Pre-implementation review recommendation:
- Run both Witness and Arbiter before Forge.
- Witness should verify:
  - package ownership split between `scribe_mcp` and `council_mcp`
  - no generated-output edits are included in source packages
  - probe/readback commands exist for each package
  - fresh-DB diagnostics are read-only
- Arbiter should verify:
  - P1 and P3 are the correct first routing gate
  - P4 cannot route before P3
  - P7 is explicitly optional and deferred until measured value exists
  - no package silently turns into migration or redesign work

Rollback and defer rules:
- If P1 does not produce durable non-null generic tool timing, stop P2, P5, and P7.
- If P3 cannot prove source parity or preserve operator overrides, stop any hook rollout and treat P4 as blocked.
- If P4 cannot distinguish hook time from Scribe time, P5 comparison output must remain advisory and must not justify config changes.
- If P6 discovers drift, emit the report and stop. Recovery or ingestion needs a separate operator-approved package.
- If P7 measurements do not show meaningful warm-path savings, defer P7 and ship only the observability packages.

---
## 9. Open Questions & Follow-Ups
<!-- ID: open_questions -->

Open but non-blocking:
- Whether readable `set_project` should eventually surface a compact timing footer or whether structured probes remain the sanctioned measurement path.
- Whether project-local tool logs are intended for active dev plans or whether repo-level tool logs plus project metadata are the preferred durable surface.
- Whether `logs analyze` should consume tool-call persistence directly or continue to derive timing only from progress-log-compatible evidence.

Deferred by design:
- Any destructive DB cleanup, historical ingestion, or auto-repair workflow.
- Any Council runtime/proxy redesign beyond hook timing and timeout hardening.
- Any broad `set_project` refactor before P1-P6 measure actual bottlenecks.

